## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to diagnose cross-region latency in a service-mmesh with HTTP/3, mTLS, and a global cache. Demands concrete steps, telemetry, and mitigations across DNS, QUIC, TLS, and cache behavior.

## Key Concepts

- HTTP/3/QUIC path and pacing characteristics across regions
- Service mesh routing and cache stampede dynamics
- DNS/SRV entropy and endpoint skew in multi-region setups
- Edge TLS termination and session reuse contention

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple percentile helper (illustrative)
function percentile(arr, p){
  if (!arr.length) return 0;
  const s = arr.slice().sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const idx = Math.floor((p/100) * (s.length - 1));
  return s[idx];
}

// Mock parsing of a cross-region latency log
const fs = require('fs');
const lines = fs.readFileSync('latency.log','utf8').trim().split('\n');
const cross = lines
  .filter(l => l.includes('"region":"cross"'))
  .map(l => JSON.parse(l).latency);
console.log([50,95,99].map(n => percentile(cross, n)));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate a cache-stampede hypothesis in production without changing code?
- What telemetry would you add to prevent similar issues in the future?
